    "create_time": 1764729350.632,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "That's cardiac drift: HR rises over a long steady effort at constant pace, from dehydration, rising core temperature, and a gradual shift in fiber recruitment. ~10% over 75 min is common, especially indoors or in heat.\n\nPractical options:\n- **Cap by HR, accept slowing:** if the goal is strict aerobic base work, ease the pace in the last 20–30 min to stay ≤148. Many coaches recommend this for base phases.\n- **Watch the drift as a fitness marker:** use decoupling (pace:HR in the first half vs second half). Under ~5% decoupling on a long easy run suggests good aerobic durability. As your base improves, drift should shrink at the same pace.\n- **Control what you can:** drink during runs longer than an hour, start a bit slower, avoid treadmill rooms without airflow.\n\nIf you're at 151 for the last 10 minutes occasionally, it isn't ruining anything. The bigger risk is the whole run creeping into the mid-150s every day."
     ]
    },
